Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
and  in this shot from ghostbusters afterlife  a terror dog stomps on paul rudd's car  but that's not cgi it's actually a  puppeteer wearing a mechanical terror  dog arm controlled by a series of cables  to make creatures like the terror dog  and this ghoulish skeleton  hyper-realistic makeup effects teams  have to juggle visual design classic  puppeteering and animatronics instead of  being an actor and giving a performance  it's getting nine people to get a  performance  making practical creatures look hyper  realistic requires plenty of trial and  error in the design phase this was  especially true for afterlife to stick  to the spirit of the original  ghostbusters the team had to modernize  the original terror dog from 1984 with  as little cgi as possible they started  with these maquettes miniature models  used to nail down a creature's look  before creating the actual on-screen  model maquettes also give the visual  effects team a real-life reference so  they can complete cg shots that are too  difficult to do practically like when  the terror dog runs through walmart this  is when aryan runs paint tests on a mock  head to determine which colors look best  on camera for example his team amped up  the lip color in the final version  and a more detailed reference head gives  a sense of what it'll look like under  movie lighting so if he's in warm light  you can tell he's more brown  but if he's in cooler light he's more  gray at the same time afterlife  animatronic designer bill sturgeon  conducted a movement test by connecting  a scaled-down 2d printout of the terror  dog to brass tubing a popsicle stick and  plastic cables this is how sturgeon  determines the creature's biggest and  most notable movements known as the  gross body move once a creature's look  and movements are solidified the makeup  effects team will then sculpt the final  design true to size for afterlife they  made a larger model of the dog out of  600 pounds of clay which served as the  mold for the actual animatronic so it  was key to get everything right down to  the crevices on its horns and skin aryan  cast the mold with foam latex a  departure from the modern standard of  silicone we opted to go with foam latex  because of the weight but it also moves  great in compression silicone would have  worked but it also would have made the  teardog about 500 pounds heavier than it  already is with the mechanics and  protective fiberglass shell the terror  dog ultimately weighed 450 pounds still  heavy but light enough to maneuver  softer foam latex wouldn't suffice for  every part of the creature like its  teeth which were made with acrylic so  they're actually hard yeah you do not  want to get bitten by one of these  the acrylic created a degree of  translucency similar to a real set of  teeth but it would be too brittle for  the dog's nails which needed to be  strong enough to endure the physical  demands of shooting so they used  urethane resin for light tough and  hollow nails they would get hit or  somebody would accidentally step on it  they wouldn't break so there's a lot of  thought that went into it these horns  were also made of the same resin as the  nails which let light in to look more  realistic on camera look at the  difference between the horns made during  paint tests and the final version it  just absorbs the light like a real horn  would the horns also needed to be sturdy  since they needed to be removable the  crew only made one terror dog  animatronic to stand in for both zuul  and vince clortho so they built the  horns with magnetic attachments which  could easily be swapped out these longer  horns were for the male and the shorter  ones were for the female terror dog but  for practical creatures to look  convincing they ultimately have to move  realistically  unlike more traditional puppeteering  animatronic work requires mechanized  motors known as servos which can be  programmed to create specific movements  instead of cables and strings they're  brought to life using controllers for  afterlife the terra dog would have to be  designed to fit all the servos inside  and there were a lot of them the terror  dog had 21 servos in its face alone to  allow for subtle expressions and one  mouth movement could take three people  to perform it because the mouth is so  big the servos have to move a lot of  material to make it look right for  example the servos needed the ability to  rotate and move a lot of skin around for  subtle movements such as blinking  squinting and sniffing so the foam latex  was much thinner in the face and neck  than the body if you know a part's gonna  move more than something else you might  make deeper wrinkles you can see that  feature in the neck which arya knew  would be heavily utilized and gave  sturgeon the ability to imitate a very  specific head turn the original puppet  did in 1984. it could even look like it  was actually eating out of a bag of dog  food during this moment the terror dog  also needed to open its mouth wide  meaning placement of the jaw's pivot  point was also key we had to figure it  out can be too high can be too low it  would look odd  [Music]  going down the rest of the animatronics  body the foam latex could be much  thicker since it had less animatronic  movement but it still required some  crucial additions sturgeon placed a  linear actuator in the beast's stomach  this specific servo would only push out  in a straight line to mimic realistic  breathing this full body shot of the  terror dog right here that's actually  cgi as aryan and his team never created  its back half for real instead the top  half was attached to a cart with two  weights on it the cart made it easier to  move the heavy puppet around so it could  be placed on the hood of a car in one  shot puppeteers will also rely on  traditional cables when the movement  calls for it like with this snarl one of  the most complicated movements for the  dog  sturgeon used cables to work alongside  the servos and the lips to really nail  the look that's actually a number of  servos working at the same time to give  it a left and right side and  snarl and in these quick shots of a  skeleton minor the ghost needed to  handle a coffee mug so creature designer  tanner white designed this cable system  that connected his hand to the miner's  hand each of the joints was the real  limit of the actual hand joint so it  didn't  move when it shouldn't so each finger  correlates to the finger movement and  since you use your own hand strength you  can grasp things a lot stronger than you  could with a radio control to grip  something like the cup here basically  you just pull all the fingers and the  wrist movement down so that he's  grasping and then if you let go  drops giving life to these creatures can  mean some very uncomfortable positions  for the puppeteers while the miners eyes  were radio controlled off camera ren had  to cable operate its brows and other  parts of its face through the bottom of  the puppet that meant crouching under  the diner counter to avoid ending up on  camera  for some moves it's easier just to  puppeteer a single part of the anatomy  on its own based on what's shown on  camera like in the close-up shot where  the terror dog stomps its foot down on  the hood of red's car to get the full  force aryan created a separate terror  dog leg made of the exact same materials  puppeteer ron binion grabbed onto a  metal armature inside the leg to move it  but the leg was so big that a human arm  couldn't reach the foot so cables were  used to give convincing ankle and toe  movements when i'm moving it i want to  move it in arcs that are similar to what  the tarot dog would do on the day of  shooting i was up pretty high same way  and trying to get myself  out of the shot i'm looking at a spot on  the car hood where where i'm aiming but  also trying to keep myself out of the  frame and  if i'm pushing down you can still see  the foot adjusting and moving and  flexing as the creature would be you  know shifting on its weight and sort of  coming down hard and just like an actor  a practical creature needs some makeup  before being camera ready that can be as  simple as a glossy shine on the skin or  some drool  so the crew applied a product called  ultra slime to the dog's lips on set so  we would do this just before the take  and set it so in other words if you  would start moving it would string and  start stretching you can see it just  starts like dripping but you got to  catch it at the right moment but that's  the fun part about it  the actors loved it i remember paul rudd  jumping up and down and gary being so so  happy to see something physically on set  which is so rare these days

